// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// markdownlint-cli2 configuration for hao-backprop-test / hello_world
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// PURPOSE
//   Makes the documentation conventions machine-checkable rather than only
//   reviewed by eye. The corpus it holds to them is every authored markdown file
//   in the repository except those under the directories named in "ignores" --
//   whatever that set happens to be when the gate runs -- and this configuration
//   is what fails when one of those files drifts away from the agreed
//   conventions. No file count or file list is recorded anywhere in here on
//   purpose: both would go stale the first time a page is added or removed.
//
//   This repository ships no style guide to inherit: there is no .editorconfig,
//   no .prettierrc, no .eslintrc* and no style document of any kind. The
//   conventions below are therefore ESTABLISHED here, and this file is what
//   makes them enforceable.
//
//   Provenance of every setting: the documentation plan's stated conventions
//   (ATX headings, one H1 per document, no skipped heading levels, an explicit
//   language hint on every fenced block) and the repository's own observable
//   state (its single existing heading is ATX). Each setting's comment names
//   which of those it comes from, so nothing here rests on an assumption.
//
// HOW IT IS RUN
//   npm run docs:lint    -> markdownlint-cli2 with NO glob arguments, so the
//                           corpus comes from the "globs" and "ignores" arrays
//                           below and from nowhere else
//   npm run docs:check   -> docs:lint followed by docs:links
//   npx markdownlint-cli2 (no arguments) -> identical coverage, same arrays
//
//   All three routes behave identically, because "docs:lint" is a bare
//   markdownlint-cli2 invocation: every glob and every exclusion comes from
//   this file (see "globs" and "ignores" below), never from the npm script.
//
// RUNTIME FLOOR FOR THIS TOOL -- NOT the application's floor
//   The service itself runs on Node >=18, and that is the floor package.json
//   declares in "engines". The documentation toolchain needs a newer runtime
//   than the service does: markdownlint-cli2 0.23.2 declares "node": ">=22",
//   and markdown-link-check 3.15.0 resolves commander 15.0.0, which declares
//   "node": ">=22.12.0". Node >=22.12.0 is therefore the floor for running the
//   lint and link gates, and package.json states it separately as
//   "devEngines.runtime" so the two floors cannot be read as one.
//
//   The two floors are also enforced differently on purpose. npm evaluates
//   "devEngines" on install and on "npm run", so its policy there is
//   "onFail": "warn": an error would abort "npm start" and "npm test" on a
//   runtime the application floor supports, which would be wrong. Every docs:*
//   script instead runs tools/check-doc-runtime.js first, and that preflight
//   fails closed -- a documentation gate has no useful behaviour below the
//   toolchain floor, so it stops rather than warning and continuing. Either way
//   the service is unaffected and still runs on Node >=18.
//
// DISCOVERY
//   markdownlint-cli2 locates configuration by EXACT filename, and it reads two
//   different families of file. They are not interchangeable:
//
//     .markdownlint-cli2.{jsonc,yaml,cjs,mjs}
//       FULL configuration. Only this family may carry CLI2 options such as the
//       "globs" and "ignores" arrays below, alongside the markdownlint rules in
//       "config". ".markdownlint-cli2.jsonc" is the first name in that list,
//       which is why this file uses it.
//
//     .markdownlint.{jsonc,json,yaml,yml,cjs,mjs}
//       Also supported, but as RULE configuration only: it supplies markdownlint
//       rules and honours "extends", and it cannot declare globs or ignores.
//       A file from this family is therefore not a substitute for this one --
//       the rules would still be applied, but nothing would tell the tool which
//       files to apply them to.
//
//   ".markdownlint-cli2.json" (no trailing "c") belongs to neither family and is
//   read by nothing. Renaming this file to that would leave the arrays below
//   unread, and with no globs from a configuration file and none on the command
//   line the tool prints its usage banner and exits 2. So the failure mode is
//   loud rather than a silently passing no-op -- but it lints nothing, and the
//   filename still matters as much as the contents.
//
//   A run that does load this file echoes the resolved set, for example
//   "Finding: **/*.md !node_modules/** !docs/api/generated/**" -- which is the
//   quickest way to confirm it was actually read.
//
// EDITING POLICY
//   Every rule switched off here is a convention the corpus stops being held
//   to. When the lint fails, fix the markdown; only revisit this file if a rule
//   genuinely conflicts with a documented requirement. Exactly two relaxations
//   are authorised (see MD013 and the note at MD033); do not add a third.
//
// VERSIONS THIS FILE WAS VERIFIED AGAINST
//   markdownlint-cli2 0.23.2 -- the DIRECT dependency, pinned exactly in
//   package.json devDependencies. It is the only one of the two this repository
//   declares.
//   markdownlint 0.41.1 -- the rule library markdownlint-cli2 depends on. It is
//   NOT a direct dependency and is not named in package.json; the version above
//   is the TRANSITIVE resolution recorded in package-lock.json, and it is what
//   `npm run docs:lint` prints on its first line. Bump it by updating the direct
//   pin and regenerating the lockfile, never by editing the lockfile by hand.
//   Every option name below exists in that pair's configuration schema.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
{
    // Schema for editor completion and validation of THIS file. Pinned to the
    // exact markdownlint-cli2 version that package.json declares directly
    // (0.23.2), so the reference cannot drift away from the tool that consumes
    // it. Note this is the CLI's schema, not the rule library's.
    "$schema": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/DavidAnson/markdownlint-cli2/v0.23.2/schema/markdownlint-cli2-config-schema.json",

    // markdownlint rule configuration. Anything not named here keeps its
    // upstream default, which "default": true holds in the enabled state.
    "config": {
        // Start from "every rule enabled". Named entries below either restate
        // a convention explicitly (so a reader sees it is deliberate) or apply
        // one of the two authorised relaxations. Nothing is disabled wholesale.
        "default": true,

        // MD001/heading-increment -- heading levels never skip (no h2 -> h4).
        // Stated explicitly because it matters most in the long README and in
        // the deeply sectioned architecture pages, where a skipped level breaks
        // the generated table of contents.
        "MD001": true,

        // MD003/heading-style -- ATX headings ("# Heading"), never setext.
        // Matches the one heading precedent the repository already has:
        // "# hao-backprop-test" (README.md:L1). Default is "consistent", which
        // would accept a setext document as long as it were uniform; "atx"
        // fixes the style across every file instead.
        "MD003": {
            "style": "atx"
        },

        // MD013/line-length -- AUTHORISED RELAXATION 1 of 2.
        //
        // The rule stays ENABLED. Only two of its sub-checks are switched off,
        // and each is switched off because the corpus cannot be written
        // correctly with it on:
        //
        //   "tables": false
        //     The corpus is table-heavy by design -- the request-matching
        //     matrix, the response-field matrix, the response-header table, the
        //     configuration-option matrix, the manifest-field table, the
        //     repository-fixture inventory and the 14-row line-by-line
        //     walkthrough table. A markdown table row cannot be wrapped: a
        //     newline ends the row and destroys the table. Exempting table rows
        //     is therefore necessary, not cosmetic.
        //
        //   "code_blocks": false
        //     Captured command output must be pasted literally -- the verbatim
        //     `node server.js` startup banner and the `curl -i` responses with
        //     their status line and headers. Rewrapping a captured transcript
        //     to fit a column budget would falsify it, and documenting output
        //     that was never produced is worse than a long line.
        //
        // "line_length" is deliberately left at its upstream default. Prose IS
        // still length-checked: markdown treats a single newline as a soft
        // wrap, so paragraphs can be wrapped without changing how they render.
        // These two exemptions are the only line-length relaxations authorised;
        // do not add a third.
        "MD013": {
            "tables": false,
            "code_blocks": false
        },

        // MD025/single-title -- exactly one H1 per document. Every page opens
        // with its own title and uses H2+ for sections thereafter.
        "MD025": true,

        // MD033/no-inline-html -- AUTHORISED RELAXATION 2 of 2, and the
        // relaxation is "leave it exactly as upstream ships it". Restated here
        // as `true` (identical to the default: enabled, with an empty
        // allowed_elements list) purely so a reader can see the position was
        // decided rather than overlooked.
        //
        // It is NOT disabled: the corpus is authored as portable markdown, so
        // raw HTML in prose stays an error. It is NOT tightened either -- no
        // allowed_elements entries are added.
        //
        // Note this does not conflict with Mermaid. markdown-it never parses
        // the contents of a fenced code block as inline HTML, so HTML inside a
        // diagram node label is invisible to this rule -- for example the label
        // `R7["R7 Comment every line<br/>(rule Sk-Rule-)"]`, taken from the
        // requirement-traceability flowchart, keeps its `<br/>` line break
        // without tripping MD033. Verified: the same `<span>` that fails in
        // prose passes inside a ```mermaid fence.
        "MD033": true,

        // MD040/fenced-code-language -- every fenced code block carries an
        // explicit language hint, and the hint must be one of the six the
        // corpus is allowed to use.
        //
        //   "allowed_languages"
        //     An explicit allow-list rather than a bare "enabled". The rule's
        //     default is an empty list, which accepts ANY word as a language
        //     and would let a stray "sh", "console" or "shell" quietly widen
        //     the vocabulary; naming the six keeps it fixed. This is not a
        //     relaxation -- it is strictly stronger than the default.
        //
        //   bash       shell commands and their captured output
        //   http       request/response exchanges
        //   javascript server.js excerpts and JSDoc examples
        //   json       package.json / jsdoc.json excerpts
        //   mermaid    all eight diagrams (DG1-DG8); the hint is exactly what
        //              makes GitHub render them natively, so it must never be
        //              stripped to satisfy a linter
        //   text       directory trees and plain terminal transcripts
        "MD040": {
            "allowed_languages": [
                "bash",
                "http",
                "javascript",
                "json",
                "mermaid",
                "text"
            ]
        },

        // MD041/first-line-heading -- every document's first line is its H1, so
        // no page starts with a badge row, a blurb or a stray paragraph.
        "MD041": true
    },

    // Files to lint. This array is the SINGLE SOURCE OF TRUTH for which files
    // the lint gate covers: `scripts.docs:lint` in package.json invokes
    // `markdownlint-cli2` with NO glob arguments, so every invocation route --
    // the npm script, a bare `npx markdownlint-cli2`, or an editor integration
    // -- resolves the corpus from right here. No other file restates this list.
    //
    // That is deliberate. markdownlint-cli2 APPENDS command-line globs to this
    // array rather than replacing it, so passing the same patterns on the
    // command line as well would express one policy in two places: the two
    // could drift, and a stale command-line argument could widen or override
    // the coverage intended here. Keep the patterns here and nowhere else.
    //
    // "**/*.md" is used in preference to a literal file list on purpose. A
    // literal list has to be edited every time a page is added, and the failure
    // mode when someone forgets is silent: the new page is simply never linted.
    // A fixed count or inventory written into a comment rots the same way, only
    // less visibly, so neither appears here.
    //
    // The invariant this array encodes instead: the recursive glob, minus the
    // directories named in "ignores" below, is exactly the set of authored
    // markdown files in the repository -- whichever files those are, at whatever
    // depth, now or after the corpus grows. Nothing has to be enumerated for a
    // new page to be covered, and no page can be missed.
    "globs": [
        "**/*.md"
    ],

    // Paths excluded from the recursive glob above. They live here and only
    // here: `scripts.docs:lint` in package.json passes no globs and no
    // exclusions of its own, so this file is the single source of truth and
    // every invocation route -- npm script, bare npx, or an editor integration
    // -- honours exactly the same set. They mirror .gitignore one-for-one.
    //
    //   node_modules/**        Materialised by `npm install` resolving the three
    //                         documentation devDependencies, after which it may
    //                         contain many third-party *.md files. None of them
    //                         are ours to lint, and leaving the directory in
    //                         would bury real findings under upstream noise.
    //
    //   docs/api/generated/**  Generated output, of both kinds: `npm run docs:api`
    //                         writes the JSDoc HTML reference here, and
    //                         `npm run docs:md` writes the markdown module
    //                         reference here. Both are derived from the JSDoc
    //                         annotations in server.js, so the files are
    //                         git-ignored build products and are never the
    //                         target of an authored link. They are excluded
    //                         because generated files must not be edited in
    //                         place: a violation reported here would be fixed in
    //                         its source -- the comments in server.js, jsdoc.json
    //                         or the generator template -- and the artefact
    //                         regenerated, so linting the artefact itself adds
    //                         nothing.
    "ignores": [
        "node_modules/**",
        "docs/api/generated/**"
    ]
}
